Tom:

Thank you for your reply to my question regarding the "isalign"
option.

I wonder if you would be familiar with the following problem I
recently ran into while porting code from another UNIX platform.
The code that I listed below yields different results from the
"cc" and "cxx" compilers (no compiler options).  It seems that
the cxx compiler has a problem with a pointer reference to
the unsigned short within a struct when the field is not on 
an even boundary and the structure is packed. "cc" works OK.
Is this a bug in cxx?

Thanks again.

Alan Rotnemer


#include <stdio.h>
#include<stdlib.h> 
#pragma pack 1
	struct aa
	{
		char cc1[7]  ;
		unsigned short us  ;
	};
 
	struct aa xaa;
void main()
{
        struct aa *pxaa, *pxaa1, *pxaa2;
        pxaa = &xaa;
	xaa.us = (unsigned short ) 299;
        printf("size of aa struct is %u\n", sizeof(struct aa));
	printf ("dot non-calloc 299 moved to %u\n",  xaa.us);
	printf ("...but using ptr ref its is %u\n",  pxaa->us);
	printf("...hex is ptr: %x, dot: %x \n", pxaa->us, xaa.us);
	pxaa->us = (unsigned short ) 299;
	printf ("pointer non-calloc 299 moved to %u\n",
		pxaa->us);
	printf ("....but using dot ref it is %u\n", xaa.us);
        pxaa1 = (struct aa *) calloc(1, sizeof(struct aa));
        pxaa1->us = 299;
	printf ("calloc ptr ref 299 moved to %u\n", 
		pxaa1->us);
}
#pragma pack 8
***************************************************************************
on cc compiler I get ...

size of aa struct is 9
dot non-calloc 299 moved to 299
..but using ptr ref its is 299
..hex is ptr: 12b, dot: 12b 
pointer non-calloc 299 moved to 299
...but using dot ref it is 299
calloc ptr ref 299 moved to 299
****************************************************************************

on cxx compiler I get..

size of aa struct is 9
dot non-calloc 299 moved to 299
..but using ptr ref its is 43
..hex is ptr: 2b, dot: 12b 
pointer non-calloc 299 moved to 43
...but using dot ref it is 299
calloc ptr ref 299 moved to 43



