Kevin Oberman wrote:
> 
> Dirk Grunwald wrote:
> >
> > I think this is incorrect. The current 'cc' is the MIPS-derived
> > compiler. cc -migrate used the GEM backend that is part of VAXC, and
> > was developed by people at Digital.
> 
> This one is really confused. It's mostly right except for the last
> sentence. The GEM compiler is the new optimization technology to which
> Digital purchased rights. With Digital UNIX V4.0, this will be the only
> C compiler. Neither cc nor cc -migrate has anything to do with VAXC.
> 
> From the man page:
> By using the -migrate compilation flag, you can take advantage of the
> DEC C compilation environment. The DEC C compiler is the same DEC C
> compiler on other platforms. The default DEC OSF/1 C compiler uses ucode
> optimization techniques and produces ucode output, while the DEC C
> compiler uses other optimizations. Both compilers however, produce
> object files that comply with the common object file format (COFF).
> 
> Under versions prior to V4.0, cc -migrate requires a developers kit PAK,
> but I understand that it will no longer require one under V4.0.
> 
> There is an entire section on the -migrate flag in the man page.

--------
OK, there is some confusion here on all sides and I'll try to explain
what is going on.  From the begining until Digital UNIX V3.2(abcde),
the default compiler has been the MIPS derived ucode compiler (ACC).  In
V1.3 we started putting the DEC C GEM based compiler on the system
under the -migrate switch.

In V4.0, both compilers will continue to ship but the DEC C compiler
will be the default and the ACC compiler will be available under the
-oldc switch.  The -migrate switch is still supported, but people 
shouldn't be using it any more as there are slight differences in
flags passed by the driver between -migrate and the V4.0 defaults.

If you are doing any development besides building your kernel you will
need to purchase the Developers' Toolkit license.  This covers both
compilers.  This will not change in V4.0.

GEM is a multi-language, multi-platform, highly optimizing back end.
It is currently used for UNIX, OpenVMS, and NT platforms and C, C++,
Fortran, Ada, Cobol, and Pascal languages, and probably a few others.
It was developed at Digital.

 -dave-

