I just experienced some wierd problems with bootp/tftp on my DEC
3000-900 server running Digital Unix v3.2d. The object was to download
a firmware update boot image to client workstations and when this is
done download a boot image for installing Digital Unix 3.2d using
RIS. In order to change the image about which image is to be
downloaded I simply changed the entry in /etc/bootptab so that it for
a particular client (which previously had downloaded the firmware
update) pointed to the installation kernel instead. When this was done
I tracked down and killed any running bootpd. I also restarted inetd.
What according to my experience now should have happened is that the
client upon a '>>> boot -protocols bootp ewa0' (AlphaStation 200)
should _not_ request the firmware update image but rather the install
kernel. It didn't. The Alphastation insisted on requesting the
image it loaded before. A powercycle of the Alphastation (to rule out
ROM bugs) didn't change anything. Only a reboot of the server helped. 

Any ideas on this fellas?

/Erik
