In article <4gip23$k7v@netnews.upenn.edu>, quon@idefix.seas.upenn.edu (Kevin Girard) writes:
<stuff deleted>
|>
|>My questions are, how do I install the new firmware? I followed the
|>DEC manual, and typed
|>
|>	#/sbin/installupdate <device>
|>
|>and got the message
|>
|>	<device> on /updmnt: No valid filesystem exists on this partition.
|>
|>Using the same command with the ECO distribution got me
|>
|>	rz0: using ULTRIX partition info found on disk
|>	rz0: using ULTRIX partition info found on disk
|>	Cannot locate update information on /updmnt.
|>
|>What is /updmnt? Am I missing something? 

I hope the documentation didn't tell you to use installupdate for a firmware
update!  That's the command to use to update the operating system.  Forget 
about the meaning of the error messages, they simply don't apply.  It sounds
like you have the Firmware CDROM.  Mount it and have a look in the DOC
directory.  It contains instructions, broken down by system type, for installing
firmware via CDROM, BOOTP or MOP.

In a nutshell, to update from the CDROM, you halt your system and then boot
from the CDROM through a command similar to the following:

>>>   boot -fl 0,80 dka400

This assumes your CD is in device dka400.  The firmware update utility will 
then start.  You'll need to read the docs I referenced for additional details.

|>
|>I also tried installing the Digital Unix v3.2C discs, and was told that
|>I needed V3.2, v3.2A or v3.2B installed before going to v3.2C, and that
|>I may update to v3.2 from either v3.0 or v3.0B.
|>
|>How many installations do I need to do to get to 3.2C?? Three? 
|>

1.2 -> 2.0 -> 3.0 -> 3.2 -> 3.2C

It's unfortunate that your system is so far out of date.  The procedure is
much less painful if the system can be kept up to date.  Just a brief word
about the reasons for the multiple updates:  when new releases come out, there
are often significant changes made and the update procedure needs to account
for them.  These changes are handled on a release to release basis, therefore,
skipping releases means that the update won't be making the changes that are
needed and the results could be fatal.

Given the long path you need to follow to get to V3.2C, you should consider a
full installation of V3.2C.  The downside, of course, is that you lose
your customizations made to files or files you added to /, usr, and var.  You 
need to weigh the time you'll need to recreate the customizations after doing
the full install versus the effort in doing 4 updates.

