Hello all,

a few days ago I posted some lines about gcc/libg++ problems
which occurred after upgrading my machine to DEC-Unix 3.2D
I got some private mail from people which have the same problems.
In the meanwhile I have fixed the problems as follows:


1st Problem:

   Warning: clog defined as GLOBAL DATA but is defined in a shared lib as a
            GLOBAL FUNC

Solution:

   I use a patched version of "libm.so" which resides in
   "/usr/local/lib/libm.so". It has the symbol `clog'
   patched out, since "clog" is not of any use it C/C++
   because the return type "double_complex" is not defined.



2nd Problem:

   /usr/ucb/ld:
   Warning: Linking some objects which contain exception information sections
        and some which do not. This may cause fatal runtime exception handling
        problems (last obj encountered without exceptions was 
        /usr/local/lib/libstdc++.a).

Solution:

   I slightly changed the gcc sources (~/gcc-2.7.2/config/alpha/*)
   to place the symbols "gcc2_compiled." and "__gnu_compiled_c" in
   the ".rconst" section. I compiled gcc and libg++ again, and all
   went good.


Now, all works fine :-).

Greetings Norbert

