Alternatively run my `autonice' daemon which progressively lowers the
priority of long-lived jobs.  You can get the latest version from:

  ftp://ftp.mrc-bbc.ox.ac.uk/pub/dec_alpha/autonice-0.4.tar.gz

For those of you already using autonice, I'm working on an enhanced
version which accepts a tiny subset of `C' as a run-time configuration
file.  This will allow configurations such as:

-- sample autonice.conf ---

/* don't to anything to root's processes */
if (user == "root") return;

if ((time >= 86400) && (nice < 20)) {

  /* kill processes more than 24 hours old that aren't nice 20 */
  kill(SIGHUP);

} else if ((time >= 3600) && (nice < 12)) {

  /* set nice 12 for processes an hour old that aren't already nice 12 */
  nice = 12;

} else if ((time >= 60) && (nice < 4)) {

  /* set nice 4 for processes older than a minute */
  nice = 4;

}

---

I'm hoping to release this version in a couple of weeks, I'll make an
official announcement when it's ready.

