dave@heffalumps.dmv.com (J. David Purnell) wrote:
>
>I need help setting up a pop mail daemon under Digital Unix.
>
>I've set up /usr/spool/pop/POP as a pop database.  The man page then tells
>me to go run popwrd to assign passwords to users in the database.  Popwrd
>complains that it can't find ~pop, which seems to suggest that it wants a
>user named pop on the system.  There's already a mail alias for user pop. 
>Do I actually have to setup a real account named pop?
>
>The popd man page seems to suggest that it will suck user mail out of
>/var/spool/mail and put it in /usr/spool/pop for pop users.  Is this
>right?
>
>
>Would anyone care to make a concise list of what I need to do to get this
>running?
>

Yes, you need a real account "pop" and it must be the owner of /var/spool/pop.
You also need a group "pop". See "man mail_manual_setup" for the detailed
step-by-step procedure.

All mail to POP users will go into /var/spool/pop. POP accounts need only an
alias in /var/adm/sendmail/aliases. They do not need separate accounts nor
does their mail ever go into /var/spool/mail as far as I know.

When you start popd, you will also probably want to start it on port 110
(POP3) instead of the default port 109. For example:

    /usr/lib/mh/popd -p 110 >/dev/null 2>&1 &

-Tom
