The Alpha was running Digital Unix V3.4D, and I think the DEC C
compiler was being used (it might have been 'gcc', I didn't run it
myself). The problem was purely numerical - a neural-network solver.
This particular problem trackes the SPEC fairly well (since it looks a
lot like ALVIN and EAR in the SPEC suite).

This particular problem tends to run very well in a small cache, and
runs well enough when compiled with 'gcc' that the author tends to use
that all the time (for portability).

In any case, the surprising point to me was that although the P6 has
zippy integer performance, the floating point performance of an Alpha
can make an Alpha/Linux system more cost effective.








