In article <4g5nr8$945@newsbf02.news.aol.com>, dflair1@aol.com (DFlair1) 
writes:
>Does anyone have experience using PCI fast-wide SCSI
>controllers for DEC Unix on Alpha? What 3rd party controllers
>are supported? We want to use fast-wide drives but don't want to pay DEC's
>very high price, considering Seagate Barracuda's.
>
>

I have an Alphaserver 8400 with 6 PCI FWD SCSI cards in. I have both DEC
rz29 and segate elite 9 drives on these busses and they work very well. 
Over here is the U.K. at least to academic sites there is not a very large 
price difference between an rz29 and a seagate barracuda.  DEC do two 
different PCI FWD SCSI cards. I have the KZPSA variety but there is now a
cheaper card as well which is O.K. if you do not want to use it for 
clustering in a ASE environment.

One thing to note is that if you put third party disks on then make sure you
add an enrty in the /sys/data/cam_data.c file for the disk and rebuild your
kernel otherwise DEC Unix will be cautious and not use SCSI Tagged Command 
Queing. Adding this enrty makes things run a whole lot faster, especially 
when talking to multiple disks on a single bus.

