I had this problem back in '94 after disabling NIS on my
machine prior to taking it off the network - I'm unsure if
this caused the problem.

Looking in /dev showed that I'd somehow lost ttyp1 - this 
needed to be remade.  I think I did this as follows:

Boot in single user mode (boot -flags s)

  mount -u /
  de /dev
  mknod ttyp1 c 6 1
  chmod 666 ttyp1

then logout.  A further reboot may be necessary.

This cured my machine; I've not seen the problem since and I 
don't know what caused it.  Hope this helps!

Mike Atkinson


