From: lueck@gurgi.zk3.dec.com (Greg Lueck Development Environment)
Subject: Re: Profiling code
Date: 1996/02/20
Message-ID: <4gcme5$c06@lead.zk3.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 140261962
distribution: world
references: <4g7n4l$87b@cherokee.advtech.uswest.com>
organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
reply-to: lueck@zk3.dec.com (Greg Lueck Development Environment)
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.unix.osf.misc


You are correct that the pixie profiler counts basic blocks to determine
an application's profile.  However, the prof command does not provide a
mechanism for viewing the raw basic block data.  You can view the
profile by procedure, source line, or assembly instruction only.

It sounds like you already have a procedure profile of your application.
You can view an assembly level profile by passing the "-asm" switch to the
profiler.  For example:

	% prof -pixie -asm myprog myprog.Addrs myprog.Counts

Since this output is so voluminous, it is often useful to limit it to a
few procedures with the "-only" switch.

You can view a source line level profile by passing either the "-lines"
or the "-heavy" switches.  For example:

	% prof -pixie -lines myprog myprog.Addrs myprog.Counts

In order for this to work, you must make sure the application was
compiled with debug level "-g1", "-g2", or "-g3".  Probably "-g1" is
what you want.  It allows all optimizations, but includes minimal
symbolic information too.  If you do not compile with a -g switch, the
compiler does not include source line information in the object file,
and the profiler is unable to display source line information.  If you
only want source line information for a few procedures, it is valid to
just compile those procedures with "-g1".

Since you do not have the V3.2C manpages loaded, you may be unaware of
the "-truecycles" switch, which may be of interest to you.  This switch
causes the profiler to include the effect of pipeline stalls in its
cycle counts.  Here is the description from the manpage:

  -truecycles [0,1,2]
      Use this flag only with the -pixie flag.  Generates more analysis of a
      program to provide a more accurate reading of cycles. The higher the
      number chosen from the arguments, the more accurate the reading,
      although the profiler will run slower. This flag is valid only in
     -pixie mode.

I hope this information is of use to you and your analysis.

