In article <4g7fvs$e2r@oac2.hsc.uth.tmc.edu>,
David S. Loose-Mitchell <dloose@farmr1.med.uth.tmc.edu> wrote:
>I recently installed a stand-alone printer ( a Lexmark Optra) that has
>quite remarkable net capabilities. Using lprsetup, getting the Alpha
>to see the printer was no (well, not too much) hassle. However the
>printer is a postscript device a obviously needs to see postscript
>input. Does anyone know how to tell lpr that a file should be
>formatted as postscript?
>
>David S. Loose-Mitchell

Well, the question is wether or not the printer knows that it should
be printing postscript or PCL (general text).  Most postscript printers
now-a-days will "Auto Detect" whether or not a document is in postscript
or is just plain text.  I have set up a Lexmark Optra LXI before, and if
I remember correctly (I may not be), it could "Auto Detect" what was
coming to it.  As a result, all you need to do is set up a remote printer
queue for it, and it should print postscript or text without a problem.

If it doesn't "Auto Detect", then you need to set up a filter that
will detect whether or not the print job is in postscript, and send
special escape codes to the printer to change the printer to postscript
mode or PCL (depending on what you're printing).

Hope this helps,

john...



