In article <4gcgqg$457@nntp.atlanta.com>, curts@compgen.com (Curt Smith) writes:
> 
> The following is quoted from a reply to my similar net questions that 
> seems to address the Alpha SMP with multi-task on shared memory accesses:
> 
>         The memory barrier instruction guarantees that all subsequent
>         loads or stores will not access memory until after all previous
>         loads and stores have accessed memory, as observed by other
>         processors. 
> 
> It was infered that there were uni-processor scenarios where memory
> barrier inst would also be [useful, necessary] ??

Yeah, stuffing data into a CSR. The CPU will collect writes to the same
location (such as stuffing a string into the FIFO of a UART) and is
free to re-order writes. To make it even more fun, the MACRO32 compiler
can re-order reads.
