writes:

|> I've tried several AVI files from the Windows For Windows CDROM 
|> with the video players in /usr/bin/mme but they don't seem
|> to play indicating that the video compression format for 
|> the AVI file is not supported.

The AlphaVCR utility included with Multimedia Services for Digital UNIX
supports AVI files containing Indeo and Motion JPEG compressed video.
For AVI files which are authored for CDROM or Internet distribution, the
two most popular video codecs are probably Indeo and Cinepak.  Unfortunately,
at the time we discussed licensing the Cinepak codec, the terms were 
unreasonable, so we didn't pursue it.  Things might have changed since
Supermac was acquired by Radius though, because the public domain movie
player, XAnim, includes Cinepak playback.  In my experience, XAnim works
well on Digital UNIX systems, so it might be a good solution for you.

If you have control over the authoring of the content, I'd recommend using
the Intel Indeo compressor for your AVI files.  This codec is comparable
to Cinepak and supported on a wide range of platforms including Windows 3.1,
Windows 95, Windows NT, and several Unix boxes (XAnim also supports it).
It is also supported under Quicktime on Macintoshes.

As you noted, AlphaVCR also supports software playback of MPEG-1 Video and
MPEG-1 Systems (audio & video) streams if you need that capability.

