In article <4f7th1$l3h@zk2nws.zko.dec.com> Paul Henderson <pgh> writes:
> The maximum # of users is currently 64,000.

Nope, it is 65536 (limits.h defines UID_MAX as USHRT_MAX that is
defined in machine/machlimits.h to be 65536U) (at least in 3.2).

Only setuid and setreuid checks the uid limits, so if you binary patch
kern_prot.o and change the "ldah at,65536(zero)" to "lda at, -1(zero)"
at the start of those functions, you can use larger uids. Freenet
Finland had to do that few weeks ago after we reached the uid 65536,
and we have been running with patched kernel since that without any
problems (there are some other limits in various systems (16 bits :
ufs (advfs is ok), cpio, restore, 18 bits : tar)).
