x-mailer: Mozilla 2.0 (X11; I; OSF1 V3.2 alpha)

Happy happy joy joy!  Our first roll-yer-own OSF/1 Alpha box came to
life last night.

The PCI33 is a Baby-AT footprint motherboard from Digital Semiconductor
which slots straight in to a standard PC chassis.  It has a 233MHz AXP
CPU, 3 PCI slots, 4 EISA slots, 4 SIMM slots, SCSI 2, floppy connector
and two serial ports on-board, with either 256Kb or 1Mb of fast cache.
Choice of OS2 or PS2 keyboard style.

One of the PCI/EISA slots is shared, so it's really only 2 PCI & 4 EISA,
or 3 PCI & 3 EISA.  The CPU is in a ZIF socket, so (theoretically)
upgradeable, although I don't know if this design will support anything
faster than 233 (as and when ...).  Was probably only to allow 166 ->
233.  The memory has to be parity RAM, so your old PC SIMMS probably
won't work in it :(.

Our prototype system has been built from scratch with a desktop
enclosure (4 or 5 drive bays depending on device height), power supply,
PCI33 board (with 1Mb bcache), 32Mb of RAM, CD-ROM, PCI Ethernet card,
VT520 terminal, 2 user OSF/1 license with "server extensions", C
Development license, and a 4Gb Barracuda for a total price of approx.
$6K.

That's right, a ready to roll 233MHz Alpha OSF (oops, Digital UNIX) box
for around $6K.

I'd like to find out if anyone else is using this configuration for OSF
(rather than Linux or NT), and ask about any experiences folk have had
with it, good or bad.  We are considering getting seriously involved
with reselling these things.  I think they'd make great low budget, high
performance web and news servers.  The only real choice is OSF v. Linux.

We spent a long time pricing out various options, and the PCI33 w/ OSF
config easily beat out an equivalent Pentium 133 w/ Solaris for price.
I hope it'll also beat it for performance, but only time will tell that.

   -- hugh

--
