RE: AXP advice on code for byte parsing??

A couple of things to consider:

> We are now fully aware of un-aligned int/long issues but surely there
> are more tricks to performing the following type of operations:

        To prevent getting the unaligned access faults, use the
        __unaligned keyword on pointer declarations that you know will
        be pointing to unaligned data.   The code generated will
        be more pesimistic than the default code but it will run several
        orders of magnitude faster than code that is getting unaligned
        access faults.

> We need to optimize the most performance out of a function that
> does mostly byte addressing and moving / converting data types.
> No consideration for CPU specific issues where given to this function
> till; bad-boy-function VS picky-as-heck-RISC-CPU face off had the CPU
> on the winning side.
>
> We are now fully aware of un-aligned int/long issues but surely there
> are more tricks to performing the following type of operations:
>
> General todo;
>
> - Convert a 400 byte record of structure ABC to 500 byte record of
>   structure DEF.
>
> - Structures ABC and DEF, have fields of ASCII, BCD, binary and oddly
>   encoded date values on all sorts of address boundaries.
>
> Question mostly pertains to:
>
> - ?? Tricks to picking up a 5 byte BCD field from struct offset = 101,
>   in the MOST CPU efficient way possible??  I.E. picking up 8 bytes at
>   at time into an auto long variable spanning the field in question??
>
> - ?? The bad-boy-var is now in an auto var, char buf, or if int, in and
>   int variable.  I suppose there's no AXP/RISC tricks at this point...
>
> - ?? Am I correct to assume that the most efficient way to write a auto
>   variable, int, to a non-aligned struct address is via memcpy()?

        For a 5-byte field, use the intrinsic version of memcpy()
        **with a size the compiler can determine at compile time**.
        That way, you'll likely get reasonable code.  To get the intrinsic
	version of memcpy(), include /usr/include/string.h and
        add -D_INTRINSIC to your compile line.  Both cc and cc -migrate
        support this.

        One "trick" (that memcpy uses for long moves) is to pick up an
        aligned piece of source, shift as needed, combine with the piece
        you picked up last time, and store it to an aligned place in the
        destination.

        If you need to expand a structure by adding a spare byte in every
        5th slot, or something, this might be a better way to structure
        the code than moving each field independently.




