Mark LoSacco wrote:
> 
> Hello,
> 
> I am working on an application running under OSF/1 V3.2 which uses
> the pthread library.  I have some questions about the way in which applications
> that use the pthread library should be compiled and linked.
> 
> The following small sample crashes in the call to pthread_mutexattr_create().
> 
> #include <pthread.h>
> #include <stdio.h>
> main() {
>    pthread_mutexattr_t x;
> 
>    pthread_mutexattr_create(&x);
> }
> 
> When built as follows, the sample crashes:
>    cc test.c -lpthreads
> 
> But when I link to the libc_r.so library, the call is successful:
>    cc test.c -lpthreads -lc_r
> 
> I would assume that libc_r is the threaded version of libc.
> Is there some way with flags to tell the compiler that it should use
> the threaded version of libc, or is linking libc_r the correct thing
> to do?
> 
> Is anyone else out there using the pthread library successfully?
> Any insights/pointers on this would be much appreciated.  Please
> email me directly at mark@bristol.com.
> 
> Thanks in advance,
> Mark
> 

The proper way to build the application is:

	cc test.c -threads

This is documented (at least) in the Guide to DECthreads, in the UNIX
appendix (most of the document is platform-independent). Unfortunately,
it didn't document the detailed effects of -threads, which turns out to
be more important than we'd originally expected...

As well as providing the proper -l list for a threaded application, the
-threads switch also adds -D_THREAD_SAFE to the command line, which
causes errno.h to generate a thread-safe definition for "errno" rather
than using the traditional "extern int errno;". If you don't compile
with this definition, your code won't work correctly in a threaded
process (at least if you try to set errno for something that was built
thread-safe, or read errno from something that was built thread-safe).

However, if you're linking a shared library (ld doesn't support
-threads), the correct -l list is:

	-lpthreads -lmach -lc_r -lc

The problem is that, while the libpthreads.so shared library will pull
in the necessary dependencies automatically when you run the program,
they'll come in AFTER all the explicitly specified libraries (this
supports the normal rules of UNIX symbol preemption on which many
programs may depend). Thus, an implictly included libmach.so and
libc_r.so will come after the libc.so library -- and both of them depend
on preempting libc.so symbols.

In Digital UNIX 4.0, there's a new switch, "-pthread", which builds a
program using the POSIX 1003.1c-1995 interfaces. The "legacy" interfaces
(cma and the POSIX draft 4 variants) are built entirely on top of the
new POSIX 1003.1c-1995 "core" thread library, libpthread.so. (I know
it's confusing to have "libpthread.so" and "libpthreads.so" -- but I
blame OSF for choosing the old name for us. :-) ) Building with -pthread
omits the legacy library so new code doesn't need to carry around the
old baggage. For shared libraries, use:

	-lpthread -lmach -lexc -lc

To link "legacy" code on Digital UNIX 4.0, use the old -threads, or:

	-lpthreads -lpthread -lmach -lexc -lc

(And always remember the -D_THREAD_SAFE [or -D_REENTRANT] if you compile
without -threads or -pthreads!)

Old binaries will work on Digital UNIX 4.0, by the way, since with a
thread-safe libc we no longer need to depend upon preempting libc
symbols; the fact that the new libraries (including the "core"
libpthread.so) will be loaded after libc is of no consequence. (The
libc_r.so "library" still exists for old binaries... but is just a link
to libc.so).

