Brent A. Conrad (baconrad@mtt.ca) wrote:
: When running the Gateway packet screening facility my host appears to be 
: dropping packets because the kernal buffer is full.

: total packets screened: 2481991
: total accepted: 2479149
: total rejected: 2018
: packets dropped:
:         because buffer was full:        824
:         because user was out of sync:   0
:         because too old:        0
: total dropped:  824

: The host machine is an Alpha running OSF1 3.2b with 32 Megs of RAM.  
: Granted the number of packets lost is not that great right now; however, 
: what will happen if the volume of network traffic increases by say 7-8 
: times?  Will adding more RAM solve the problem?

: Thanks in advance,
:               Brent A. Conrad   

1. You should increase

   SCREEN_MAXPEND

   in /sys/data/gw_screen_data.c and rebuild your system.
   SCREEN_MAXPEND defaults to 32, which seems to be too small when
   the machine is flooded with packets (i.e. UDP).

2. Will adding more RAM solve the problem?
   Partially, the kernel needs more RAM to hold SCREEN_MAXPEND packets,
   32 MB is very poor, even for a machine without a packet screen.
   I think there is also a limit on the number of packets an ALPHA
   workstation  can handle, you can't compare it's performance
   (based on the number of packets it can switch) with the
   performance of a router.

