I recently posted the same request for dos utils, and courtesy 
of: aharon@healdb.matat.health.gov.il (Israeli Health Ministry)
received a prompt solution that took ~5 minutes to implement
and test.

If you have the DEC OSF/1 Alpha Freeware CD there is a
utility suite, mtools (~/KITS/SOURCE/MTOOLS/MTOOLS/MTOOLS_2.TAR;1)
that can be be trivially modified to compile on an DUX/Alpha
box.  The non-unix file naming conventions result from the multiple
xplatform targets for this distribution -- you can follow 
directions for freeware software installation and in a few minutes
normalized links will be created which follow standard unix naming 
conventions.  This Freeware package is also available online 
courtesy of:

                  munroe@acornsw.com  (Acorn Software, Inc.)


at the following sites


WWW site:
   URL:            http://www.acornsw.com/
   IP#:            140.186.88.9

ftp site:      
   URL:            ftp://flash.acornsw.com/
   site:           flash.acornsw.com
   IP#:            140.186.88.9
   user:           anonymous

Install the mtools tar file on a rw disk in an appropriate
target directory, e.g.

the following modifications will allow for compilation on an alpha box:


Make a copy of the original Makefile 

  cp ~/mtools-2.0.7/Makefile ~/mtools-2.0.7/Makefile.orig

insert the following two lines into the Makefile up top
near the other platform dependent CFLAG assignments.

  # for Alpha/OSF
  CFLAGS = -O -DBSD -DFLOCK
  
be sure all other CFLAG assignments other than the one you've
inserted are commented out via preceding #

insert the following #ifdef, structure decalration construct in
the source file devices.c: (right above #ifdef SPARC_ODD...) 

 #ifdef __alpha
 struct device devices[] = {
 	{'A', "/dev/rfd0c", 0L, 12, 0, (int (*) ()) 0, 80, 2, 18},
 	{'A', "/dev/rfd0c", 0L, 12, 0, (int (*) ()) 0, 80, 2, 9},
 	{'\0', (char *) NULL, 0L, 0, 0, (int (*) ()) 0, 0, 0, 0}
 };
 #endif /* __alpha */

You may optionally change the MANDIR and DESTDIR definitions in
the Makefile to satisfy conventions/requirements on the system
you're installing this on...

just compile via

 make


there you have it, a nice set of tools for handling dos files,
disks, and conventions on your screaming DUX alpha box.

btw -- there are man pages for all the commands which are
essentially all of the ole' dos commands (copy, dir, format, etc...)
with an m prefix (mcopy, mdir, mformat, metc...). 

have fun...

tjm (mickoltj@halcyon.com)

p.s. remember to thank the US Army and Israeli Ministry of Health
     for making this all possible...

