sender: news@info.physics.utoronto.ca (System Administrator)
references: <DMD7Gy.FDJ@uns.bris.ac.uk>
organization: Canadian Institute for Theoretical Astrophysics
reply-to: mark@cita.utoronto.ca
newsgroups: comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec,comp.unix.osf.osf1

[ Tim Llewellyn ]

| We just received out VMS 7.0 kit, and discovered that turbochannel
| PXG boards are not supported for VMS 7.0 (and neither for Digital Unix
| 4.0 either apparently).
|
| So, we can put off upgrading to 7.0 for a while, but as we have a 3000-400
| with PXG running VMS I'd be interested to know (1) why is support being
| withdrawn (2) are there any upgrade deals (eg with DEQUNA->DELQA).

If this is really true (as opposed to some {botch,oversight} with these new
releases, which I suppose isn't inconceivable), my opinion of Digital (which
has been more or less on a steadily monotonic upward trend since the arrival
of the first Alpha systems) is about to take an abrupt nosedive back in the
direction of where it was back in the "Yes, we will provide and support OSF
on our MIPS-based machines ... Oops, no, we won't after all" days.

Not because we have any systems with PXG boards.  (We considered getting it
for one or two systems, but finally decided not to.)  But because it would
be an indication that the company can't be counted on to provide long-term
support for its products.

I realize that no company can be expected to support forever every hardware
product it's ever produced.  But in the case of the turbochannel AXP boxes,
it's been barely three years since the *first* of these products were brand
spanking new, and their direct descendents (i.e. the 3000/[6789]00 systems)
are much more recent than that.

To compare with one other vendor:  SGI has announced that IRIX 5.3 will be
the last release which will run on their Personal IRIS series machines (the
4D/xx systems).  But some of these date back seven years or more, and we've
always been able to bring up IRIX without problem on even our oldest system
from SGI, with no question that it would support whatever hardware options
we'd bought from them.

It strikes me as perfectly reasonable to phase out support for a seven year
old machine.  But for Digital to eliminate support for something that's two
or three years old would be unconscionable.

I'm eagerly awaiting a reply from someone at DEC, either to tell us that PXG
boards will be supported, or to explain to us why a decision was made not to
support them.  In the latter case, how can I be sure that some hardware that
I *do* have will still be supported in, say, DU 5.0?  How can I confidently
buy anything from Digital again, if its support life cycle might be as short
as two years?

