[ Peter David ]

| 	Just to clarify this issue, DEC announced when it introduced
| the first alpha systems that the PXG and PXG+ boards would not have
| continued support.   [ ... ]
|
| 	DEC has been telling the PXG users for a LONG time that the
| boards would not have continued support.  I am sorry that it comes as
| a surprise to some users, but it is not a change of direction by DEC.
| They have been very consistent on this issue.

Well, if all that were true, I admit it would put a different spin on
things.  But through what channel(s) did Digital communicate the fact
that the PXG boards would have limited support?  It's clear that you
knew, but it's just as clear that it seems to have come as a surprise
to Tim Llewellyn; and I had never heard about this, either.  When we
were considering buying the PXG+, no mention was ever made about any
lack of commitment to long-term support.  And the person who was our
sales rep at the time is pretty sharp, tends to stay on top of issues
of this sort, and is quite diligent about making sure that customers
are well informed.  So I suspect that, if in fact Digital really did
have a short-lifetime policy on the PXG hardware, there was likely an
internal communication failure within the company.  Or maybe the word
didn't propagate outside the US (I'm in Canada, and Mr. Llewellyn is
in the UK).

It may also be worth noting that both the April 1993 and February 1994
editions of Digital's Systems and Options Catalog list the PXG boards,
but no mention is made anywhere (at least that I can find) that these
options will cease to be supported any earlier than the Alpha systems
into which they'd be installed.  If these boards were actually marked
for possible early demise, wouldn't it have been appropriate to have
said something about this in the S&O Catalog?

|         As an aside, those PXG boards are significantly slower than
| any of the replacement boards offered by DEC.  Video products have
| improved significantly since the days of PXG+ turbo boards.

Quite true, but irrelevant.  The same thing could be said of the AXP
systems themselves.  But I'd be pretty annoyed if Digital announced
that DU 4.0 wouldn't run on my 3000/500 systems, simply because they
have been superseded by much better machines.

