Subject: Re: HELP: How to get hard disk information on OSF1
Date: 1996/02/02
Message-ID: <4eu485$dh9@maverick.tad.eds.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 137734135
references: <4em1th$eo8@jethro.Corp.Sun.COM>
content-type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
organization: EDS
x-url: news:4em1th$eo8@jethro.Corp.Sun.COM
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1
x-mailer: Mozilla 1.12 (X11; I; OSF1 V3.0 alpha)

You need to use the disklabel command on OSF/1. Its in /sbin directory.
The command goes something like this: /sbin/disklabel -r /dev/rre1a.  You have
to use the raw disk name and also the first partition.  The only problem is
you must be root to run the command.  This is because you can rewrite the
disklabel and therefore change the partitions on the disk.  If you use the df 
command to display the file systems the typical DEC naming convention uses the 
2nd and third positions as the type of disk (rz, re ) and the fourth positon
is a number which usually equates to the SCSI id.

On Ultrix the /etc/chpt -q command gives you the partions but not as much info
as the disklabel on OSF.  Again, you must be root to execute it.

Hope this helps.

Bill Reid
Electronic Data Systems




