In article <4er8ns$1sa@nntp.atlanta.com>, Curt Smith <curts@compgen.com> wrote:
>I'm looking for fax server packages other than hylafax, both
>commercial and other?
>
>I have a customer who has asked for this feature set as an
>additional system to our regular product line.  So we can put this
>feature set on most any medium sized box and OS (Unix).
>
>I have hylafax 3.0.1 and I asking if there are any other
>packages out there I should look at.

GNU has a fax server/client system.  Look in prep.ai.mit.edu in the 
/pub/gnu directory.  It is not as robust as Hylafax (which I have found 
to be the BEST fax software out there), but it may be just the right 
thing for you and your customer.

If your problem with Hylafax is the redistribution rights (i.e. you want 
to bundle it with one of your products), GNU fax may not work for you as 
well, depends on how deeply you want to ensnare yourself with their GPL.

If this is so, then there are several others that I know of -- efax, 
mgetty+sendfax, qfax, and vfax, to name a few.  Look at sunsite.unc.edu 
in /pub/Linux/apps/comm/fax.  Yes, this is the ftp site for LINUX 
programs, but they will probably tell you where the original 
non-Linux-mangled packages originally came from (I have no idea).  None 
of these packages are as robust as hylafax is, IMHO.  Hylafax's and GNU 
fax's strengths are that it is especially suited for networks that share 
one or more fax modems, and want their users to be able to fax from their 
workstation; all the other smaller packages are primarily meant for 
single-user machines.
