In article <4eptjt$e60@gandalf.compumedia.com>, kkirk@compumedia.com writes:
>
>I've heard that to add a new tape or disk drive to a DEC Unix
>machine requires that the system be regenned/kernal rebuilt/rebooted.
>Is this true?

I think one of the system management documents does say this, but
is isn't *necessary* for just a new SCSI device.  Adding a new
SCSI adapter or some other new driver, yes.  Added a new MSCP
disk on a DEC 7000, maybe.  You can generalize the configuration
file to recognize nearly any new MSCP disk or tape that appears.
I think the RE driver the RAID backplane controllers will also
dynamically recognize new units.

So, to add a new device:

1.  If you have to unterminate a SCSI bus to add the device
    it would be wise to shutdown.

2.  Add the device.  Use the console to verify the device
    can be seen.

3.  Boot.  Watch the boot messages for the new device.

4.  After the system has booted, login as root and create
    the special device files.  This why watching the boot
    messages was recommended, so you'd know the device name.
    Typically these have the form:

	rz# - SCSI disk, # = (bus * 8) + target-id
	tz# - SCSI tape, # = (bus * 8) + target-id
	re# - RAID back-plane controller, # = unit number
	ra# - MSCP disk, # = unit number (0-255)
	tms# - TMSCP tape, # = unit number (0-31)

5.  For disks, label it.  This will write the default partition
    table and the geometry information.  When the disk is well
    behaved, the disklabel command can get the geometry from
    the disk and doesn't need a disktab entry.

6.  Start using it.  There's a lot of variety here, so no one
    simple command is appropriate.  To use it with LSM you'll
    need to encapsulate it and write the LSM private areas.
    To put a UFS on it, you'll want to use newfs(8).  To use
    it with AdvFS you'll want to use mkdmn or addvol.
>
