In article <310E7ECD.3A1@oac.hsc.uth.tmc.edu>, "Steven E.Newton" <snewton@oac.hsc.uth.tmc.edu> writes:
>
>What nlist symbols point to the load average of a running kernel
>on 3.2?  I have tried avenrun but the values I get are extremely
>small, on the order of N^-321.

I checked with dbx as well and it believed that avenrun was a single
integer:

(dbx) whatis avenrun
int avenrun;

Searching the source files of V3.2A didn't show it declared anywhere,
but looking through the .h files show a:

extern long avenrun[3] ;

in <sys/kernel.h>.  Assuming this is right it is probably a fixed
point number.  A closer look at kernel.h, suggests the scale is 1000.
There was also a mention of avenrun in <sys/table.h> suggesting that
the table(2) interface allows collecting it.  Since this is what *I*
use in Monitor, it is probably a safer interface.
>
>Or is there another programmatic interface to get the load averages?

I use table(2) with TBL_LOADAVE.  Recent versions include a manual
page for the table(2) call.
