In article <4eoqjg$o72@uranium.chem>,
Andrew Benson <bensona@metamor.com> wrote:
>
>	Is it normal to have processes like dxterm and dxmail 
>apparrently using tons of memory (virtual)?
....
>	Any thoughts or suggestions, anyone?

Sure, dump dxterm in favor of its smaller cousin, xterm.  ps from an
alphastation running 3.2C:
root      1102  0.0  0.4 3.95M 1.1M ??       I      Jan 19     0:08.97 xterm -font 
root       598  0.0  0.2 3.30M 464K ??       I      Jan 19     0:06.27 xterm -font

Another thing that you might want to consider if you're interested in
saving memory is to stop using dxsession & mwm.  IMHO, dxsession
pretty much useless if you take a few hours to learn how xdm works &
to configure your window manager decently.  For a window manager, I'd
recommend the supplied twm, or building your own ctwm from source, but
that's a religious thing.

One of the very nice features of Digital UNIX is that Digital ships a
nearly standard xdm, any book about administering X-windows should
guide you through creating a ~/.xsession script, etc.

Never though I'd hear myself calling xterm 'small' ;-)

